course: everyday
lesson: 351
title: Homework, practice and the team counting on you
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Josh in Leeds was meant to take the bins out on Thursday night, but it was cold and he stayed on the sofa. The bin men came and went; for two weeks the full bin stank by the back door and the whole family smelled it. Nobody shouted much, but Dad said quietly «That was your one job, mate». Josh said sorry and took the bins out for the next two weeks without being asked.
explain: Ava in Ohio did not do her part of the group project on volcanoes because she thought the others would cover it. They could not finish the poster, and all four of them got a lower mark. Two friends did not sit with her at lunch for a few days. She said sorry to the group, stayed in at break to make the missing part, and asked the teacher if they could hand in the finished poster late.
explain: Laziness rarely hurts only the lazy one. A missed turn, a part not learned, a dog not walked — someone else pays: the family, the team, the animal.
explain: More skipped jobs: Leo could not be bothered to hang up his wet towel, so it lay damp and smelly on the floor. Mia played her game instead of walking the dog, and the dog had an accident on the kitchen floor; she cleaned it up herself. Noah did not make his bed for a week and could not find his school things. Lily did not learn her lines and froze on stage; she said sorry and learned them for the next show.
explain: The consequences are not always the same. Sometimes nobody notices and the child thinks it is fine; sometimes there is a bad mark, a row at home or a lost treat. Getting away with it once does not make it right — the job was still not done.
fact: When one person in a team does not do their part, the whole team usually suffers.
fact: A dog that is not walked may have an accident in the house, because it cannot wait forever.
fact: A wet towel left on the floor stays damp, and damp carpet and cloth start to smell and can grow mould.
fact: Reading the instructions first usually saves time, because mistakes made early can mean starting again.
fact: Pretending to be asleep to get out of a job is a kind of lie, even without words.
fact: Small jobs done every day, like making your bed, are easier than a big mess cleaned once a week.
fact: Actors in a play rely on each other's lines; if one forgets, the others are left stuck on stage.
fact: Saying sorry is the start; doing the missed job, or extra, is what puts it right.
fact: Getting away with skipping a job does not mean it was fine; someone else often did it for you.
fact: A punishment like losing screen time is a consequence of what you did, not revenge from your parents.
exercise: Josh skipped taking the bins out because it was cold. Why did it matter to the whole family? | The bin stayed full for two weeks and smelled | The bin men took it anyway from the back garden | Only Josh noticed the bin | Bins never smell in winter
exercise: Ava thought the others would do her part of the group project. What happened? | The group could not finish and all got a lower mark | The teacher gave her the top mark for the group's poster | The others were glad to do it | Nobody noticed the missing part
exercise: What would best put right Ava's missing part of the project? | Say sorry and do the missing work, even at break | Blame the group for not reminding her enough times | Say the project was boring anyway | Ask the teacher to mark only her own name
exercise: Why did Leo leave his wet towel on the floor every day? | He could not be bothered to hang it up | Mum had told him to leave it there to dry | The towel rail in the bathroom was broken | He was using it to dry the wet bathroom carpet
exercise: What happens to a wet towel left on the floor day after day? | It stays damp and starts to smell | It dries faster than on a rail | It becomes softer and cleaner | Nothing, towels never smell
exercise: Mia did not walk the dog on her turn because she wanted to finish a game. The dog had an accident on the kitchen floor. Why? | A dog cannot wait forever to go outside | Dogs only go indoors to annoy the people they live with | The dog was angry with Mia | The dog had eaten too much
exercise: What is the fairest thing for Mia to do after the dog's accident? | Clean it up herself and walk the dog on time | Say the dog is just badly trained and naughty | Leave it for Mum, because she is faster at cleaning | Swap the dog's walks with her brother forever
exercise: Sam started building his model without reading the instructions. It fell apart. What was the wise choice? | Read the instructions first, then build | Build faster so that it all holds together | Use more glue and skip the instructions altogether | Give up on making models for good, they're too hard
exercise: Ruby pretended to be asleep so she would not have to help unpack the shopping. Why is that a problem? | It is a lie, and Mum did the work alone | Sleeping in the day is against the rules | Shopping should never be unpacked by children | Mum enjoys unpacking the shopping on her own
exercise: Why does Mum still care if Ruby only pretended and said nothing untrue? | Pretending is a way of lying without words | Ruby should simply have pretended much better | Mum just likes to argue | Pretending is always worse than stealing
exercise: Noah did not make his bed for a week and could not find his school things. What was the lesson? | Small jobs done every day keep things easy | Beds only need making once a month, when sheets are changed | Mess helps you find things faster | Tidy rooms are only for guests
exercise: Lily did not learn her lines for the school play. On stage she froze. Who else was let down? | The other actors who were waiting for her lines | Just the teacher, who had a copy of the whole script | Nobody, it was only her part | Only the person selling tickets
exercise: What put Lily's mistake right? | Saying sorry and learning her lines for the next show | Asking to be a tree with no lines at all in the next show | Saying the script was too long | Promising never to be in a play again
exercise: Josh's sister says «you skipped the bins twice before and nothing happened». What is true? | He was lucky; the job still was not done | It proves skipping is safe forever | It means the bins do not matter | It means bin day is not real
exercise: After the bins, Dad took Josh's games console for the weekend and Josh said «that's not fair, it's revenge». Which is closer to the truth? | It is a consequence of a skipped job, not revenge | Dad only wanted the console for himself in the evenings | Consoles are always taken on Thursdays | Losing it has nothing to do with the bins
exercise: Josh sulked and slammed his door, then later came down. What is the wise way to make up? | Say sorry and do his turn without being asked | Make his sister take the bins out, it's her turn anyway | Keep sulking until Dad gives in | Pretend nothing happened at all
test: oi it's ur turn for the bins and it's FREEZING, just leave it, they'll come next week | The bin will overflow and smell; take it out now | Leave it, bins can wait weeks, the bin men always come | Ask the bin men to come in | Hide the rubbish in the garage
test: group chat: «Ava hasn't done her bit AGAIN, we're all gonna get a C cos of her». What should Ava do? | Own up, say sorry and do her part now | Leave the chat and say nothing | Say she was ill when she wasn't | Tell them their parts were worse
test: my brother says if u fake being asleep mum does the shopping herself, works every time. good plan? | No, it's a lie and leaves Mum doing it all | Yes, if you snore a bit it's totally believable | Yes, Mum likes unpacking | Only on Saturdays
test: Mum texts: «Dog's done a wee on the kitchen floor. Whose turn was it???» It was yours. Best reply? | «Mine, sorry, I'll clean it and walk him now» | «Not mine, ask Tom, he had him after school» | «He's just a bad dog» | Don't reply and hope she forgets
test: «didn't learn my lines, i'll just make it up on the night lol». What will probably happen? | The others won't get their cues and the scene falls apart | It will be the best show ever, because nobody will know what is coming next | Nobody will notice anything | The teacher will cancel the play
test: I skipped walking the dog last week and nobody noticed. «it worked last time, so it's fine.» Which is right? | It was not fine; the dog still needed the walk | Only skip it when it rains, dogs hate getting wet | It's fine if nobody notices | Dogs don't need walks really
test: Dad: «Towel. On. The. Floor. Again.» What's the real problem with leaving it there? | It stays damp, smells and makes more washing | Dad just likes neat floors and shiny bathrooms | Towels get lonely on rails | There is no real problem
test: «just start building, instructions are for babies». Which is true? | Reading first usually saves time and mistakes | Reading first always takes longer than just starting | Instructions are always wrong | Experts never read instructions
test: Your little sister tells Mum: «He's not made his bed all week AND his room stinks». She is right. What now? | Admit it and tidy up properly | Tell Mum she's lying about the mess | Tidy only the bit Mum can see from the door | Lock the door so that nobody looks inside
test: Coach: «Four of you didn't turn up to practice and we lost by six». What's the lesson? | A team needs everyone to do their part | Practice never changes a match | Losing by six is just bad luck | Only the captain needs to practise
test: «Mum took my phone for a week cos of the bins, she just hates me». Which is closer to the truth? | It's a consequence of the skipped job, not hate | She took it to use it herself while he was at school | It's a random punishment | Phones are always taken on bin day
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the omission of one member's portion of a group assignment is detrimental to the group. | The group cannot complete the work and all receive a lower mark | Only the absent member is penalised | Teachers do not mark group work | Group assignments are optional
exam: It is given that a child has omitted a domestic duty on several occasions without detection. Select the response consistent with sound reasoning. | Prior lack of detection does not render the omission acceptable | Repeated success proves the duty unnecessary and therefore removes the obligation | The duty lapses after three omissions | Detection alone determines wrongdoing
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the feigning of sleep in order to avoid a household task? | It is a form of deception that transfers the labour to another | It is an acceptable form of rest | It is required by household rules | It has no consequence for others
exam: State the measure most consistent with making amends for a neglected turn in a family rota. | An apology followed by performance of the duty, and extra if possible | A written complaint regarding the rota | A request that the rota be abolished | Silence until the matter is forgotten
